format: meaning/draft-1
id: operations
name: IT operations
description: >-
  Running a service after it is built: support tickets, incidents, problems,
  changes, service levels and the response around them.
license: CC0-1.0
concepts:
  - id: support-tier
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Support tier
    synonyms:
      en: [support level, line of support]
    description: >-
      The depth of a support response. Level 1 takes the report, applies a
      known fix or a runbook, and passes on what it cannot close. Level 2
      investigates further, often the people who operate the application.
      Level 3 is specialist depth: a change to the software, a vendor, or
      the team that builds it. Organizations draw those boundaries
      differently, and some add another tier, so the three levels are the
      usual case rather than a closed list. A tier is not an on-call
      rotation, which says who is reachable now, and not a priority, which
      says how soon a ticket should be worked.

  - id: support-tier-name
    kind: attribute
    of: support-tier
    labels:
      en: Support tier name
    description: >-
      The name this organization uses for the tier, such as Level 1, L2, or
      a vendor's own name. The name is not a priority.

  - id: support-ticket
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Support ticket
    synonyms:
      en: [ticket, support case]
    description: >-
      A tracked request for attention from the people who operate a service.
      An incident and a service request are kinds of support ticket. A
      problem is not one: it follows a cause across incidents rather than
      one report. A ticket is not a work item on a delivery backlog, though
      repairing the cause may add one.

  - id: support-ticket-assignee
    kind: attribute
    of: support-ticket
    labels:
      en: Support ticket assignee
    description: A person currently responsible for the ticket.
    values-of: person

  - id: support-ticket-assignee-team
    kind: attribute
    of: support-ticket
    labels:
      en: Support ticket assignee team
    description: A team currently responsible for the ticket.
    values-of: team

  - id: support-ticket-tier
    kind: attribute
    of: support-ticket
    labels:
      en: Support ticket tier
    description: >-
      The support tier currently responsible for the ticket. An escalation
      moves the ticket from one tier to another. The tier is not the
      ticket's priority.
    values-of: support-tier

  - id: incident
    kind: entity
    extends: support-ticket
    labels:
      en: Incident
    synonyms:
      en: [service incident, production incident]
    description: >-
      An unplanned interruption to a service, or a reduction in its quality.
      The incident is that interruption. Restoring the service is why it is
      handled, and can finish before the cause is understood. A problem is
      the cause, or a potential cause, and can sit behind several incidents.
      A service request asks for a service action already agreed as normal
      delivery; nothing has to have failed. A bug is a flaw recorded as
      delivery work and may never reach a running service. An incident can
      also come from configuration, capacity, or a dependency, with no bug.
      A change request proposes a modification. An emergency change may be
      what restores the service; the request is still not the incident.

  - id: incident-severity
    kind: attribute
    of: incident
    labels:
      en: Incident severity
    description: >-
      How badly the service is impaired, on the scale the organization uses
      for operations. A low number is not universally the worst grade.
      Severity is not impact, which is the business effect, and not urgency,
      which is how soon restoration is needed. It is not priority. Priority
      is the order of work and can diverge from the grade: a severe fault in
      a service with no users until morning is not the same queue position
      as a moderate fault that blocks today's payroll. ITIL names impact,
      urgency, and priority for this ordering, and does not define severity
      as that order.

  - id: incident-impact
    kind: attribute
    of: incident
    labels:
      en: Incident impact
    description: >-
      The effect of this incident on users or on a business process, such as
      which work is blocked. Impact is not urgency and not priority. It is
      not severity, the operations grade of the impairment. Teams often
      align the grade and the effect, and the two can still differ.

  - id: incident-urgency
    kind: attribute
    of: incident
    labels:
      en: Incident urgency
    description: >-
      How quickly the business needs the service restored. Urgency is not
      impact. A severe impairment of a service that nobody needs until
      morning can have low urgency. Practices often suggest a priority from
      impact and urgency. Incident priority stores the order that was
      chosen, including when a person overrides that suggestion.

  - id: incident-priority
    kind: attribute
    of: incident
    labels:
      en: Incident priority
    description: >-
      The order in which responders work this incident. It is often chosen
      from impact and urgency, and a person can override that suggestion.
      The attribute stores the resulting order, not the impact and not the
      urgency. It is not severity, which grades the impairment, and it is
      not the support tier that holds the ticket.

  - id: incident-status
    kind: attribute
    of: incident
    labels:
      en: Incident status
    description: >-
      Where this incident sits between being recorded and being closed.
      Practices often distinguish open, restored and resolved, but the names
      belong to the practice. Status is not severity. Resolved, on the
      record, can differ from restored, on the service.

  - id: incident-affected-service
    kind: attribute
    of: incident
    labels:
      en: Incident affected service
    description: >-
      The service whose quality is interrupted or reduced. A failing
      software component may be what broke the service; the service is still
      what the users were promised.
    values-of: service

  - id: incident-opened-at
    kind: attribute
    of: incident
    labels:
      en: Incident opened at
    description: >-
      The instant the incident was recorded. The service may have failed
      earlier; that instant is the incident failure time when it is known.
      This instant is not when the service was restored and not when a
      person acknowledged an alert.

  - id: incident-resolved-at
    kind: attribute
    of: incident
    labels:
      en: Incident resolved at
    description: >-
      The instant the incident record was marked resolved, often because a
      workaround or a fix is in place. That can be earlier or later than the
      instant the service was restored.

  - id: incident-restored-at
    kind: attribute
    of: incident
    labels:
      en: Incident restored at
    description: >-
      The instant the service met its success criterion again. Mean time to
      restore ends at this instant, not at the resolved time of the record
      and not at the time an alert was acknowledged.

  - id: incident-failure-at
    kind: attribute
    of: incident
    labels:
      en: Incident failure at
    description: >-
      The instant the service was interrupted or its quality dropped, when
      that instant is known. The record may have been opened later. Mean
      time to restore is measured from this instant, not from the opened
      time and not from acknowledgement of an alert.

  - id: incident-workaround
    kind: attribute
    of: incident
    labels:
      en: Incident workaround
    description: >-
      A way to reduce or avoid the impact of this incident without removing
      the cause. It may restore the service for now. It can be found before
      any problem has been analysed. It is not the root cause and not a
      permanent removal of the cause.

  - id: incident-related-bug
    kind: attribute
    of: incident
    labels:
      en: Incident related bug
    description: >-
      A bug recorded as a cause of this incident, when the harm comes from a
      software flaw. The attribute is absent when the cause is something
      else. Linking the two does not make the incident a bug or the bug an
      incident.
    values-of: bug

  - id: service-request
    kind: entity
    extends: support-ticket
    labels:
      en: Service request
    synonyms:
      en: [request for service, service catalog request]
    description: >-
      A request from a user, or from that user's authorized representative,
      that starts a service action already agreed as a normal part of
      delivery, such as granting access or supplying information. Nothing
      has to have failed, so a service request is not an incident. It is not
      a change request. A standard change, pre-authorized for that kind of
      request, may be how the request is fulfilled. The request and the
      change stay different records.

  - id: problem
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Problem
    synonyms:
      en: [problem record]
    description: >-
      A cause, or potential cause, of one or more incidents. The work is to
      understand that cause and to make a repeat less likely or less
      harmful. Restoring the service belongs to the incident and can finish
      while the problem stays open. A problem is not a change request. A
      change is one way the cause might be removed, and the request for that
      change is a separate record. When the problem has been analysed and is
      not yet resolved, it is a known error. A documented workaround is not
      what makes it one. A workaround can be recorded earlier.

  - id: problem-root-cause
    kind: attribute
    of: problem
    labels:
      en: Problem root cause
    synonyms:
      en: [root cause]
    description: >-
      The cause the investigation has settled on, when it has one. A known
      error means the problem has been analysed and is not yet resolved; the
      root cause can still be only partly identified. The cause is not the
      workaround that reduces the effect.

  - id: problem-related-incident
    kind: attribute
    of: problem
    labels:
      en: Problem related incident
    description: >-
      An incident this problem is taken to explain. One problem can sit
      behind several incidents. The incident remains the record of restoring
      the service.
    values-of: incident

  - id: known-error
    kind: entity
    extends: problem
    labels:
      en: Known error
    synonyms:
      en: [known error record]
    description: >-
      A problem that has been analysed and has not been resolved. Analysis
      is what distinguishes it from a problem that is still unexamined. A
      workaround is often recorded with it and is not required for this
      status. The cause can still be in the service, so a known error is not
      a completed fix, not an incident, and not a runbook. It is not the
      ITIL word error, which names a flaw or vulnerability that may cause
      incidents. A runbook may tell a responder to apply a workaround.

  - id: problem-workaround
    kind: attribute
    of: problem
    labels:
      en: Problem workaround
    description: >-
      A way to reduce or eliminate the impact of this problem when a full
      resolution is not yet available. It can be recorded before the problem
      has been analysed far enough to be a known error. It is not the root
      cause. A runbook may quote it. The workaround is not the runbook. An
      incident can carry a workaround of its own before a problem is opened.

  - id: change-request
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Change request
    synonyms:
      en: [request for change]
    description: >-
      A request for change: the recorded proposal to add, modify, or remove
      something that could affect a service, used so the risk can be judged
      before the change is made. The change itself is that addition,
      modification, or removal. This record is the proposal, not the
      incident it may help restore and not the problem whose cause it may
      remove. An emergency change is often how a service is restored; the
      request is still not the incident. It is not a release and not a
      deployment. A release is a version made available for use. A
      deployment puts a build into an environment, which is one way a change
      is carried out. A standard change may fulfill a service request. The
      request and the change stay different records.

  - id: change-request-type
    kind: attribute
    of: change-request
    labels:
      en: Change request type
    description: >-
      How this change is authorized. A standard change is low-risk,
      pre-authorized, and fully documented, so it needs no further
      authorization. A normal change is assessed and authorized before it
      goes ahead. An emergency change must be introduced as soon as
      possible, often to restore a service. The words belong to the
      practice. The type is not the risk grade and not the deployment that
      carries the change out.

  - id: change-request-risk
    kind: attribute
    of: change-request
    labels:
      en: Change request risk
    description: >-
      The assessed chance that this change harms the service, and how bad
      that harm would be, used to choose how the change is approved. It is
      not incident severity and not bug severity.

  - id: change-request-problem
    kind: attribute
    of: change-request
    labels:
      en: Change request problem
    description: >-
      A problem this change is intended to remove or reduce. The change
      request remains a proposal; accepting it is not the same as closing
      the problem.
    values-of: problem

  - id: escalation
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Escalation
    synonyms:
      en: [support escalation]
    description: >-
      Sharing awareness of a support ticket, or transferring its ownership:
      to a deeper support tier, to another team, or to a manager. It happens
      at a time. It is not an event in this graph. An event here is an
      occurrence or a gathering at a time and place. An escalation is not a
      change of priority and not a change request. Moving from level 1 to
      level 2 is an escalation, and so is moving to level 3. The tiers are
      support tiers, not priorities.

  - id: escalation-support-ticket
    kind: attribute
    of: escalation
    labels:
      en: Escalation support ticket
    description: The support ticket being handed on.
    values-of: support-ticket

  - id: escalation-from-tier
    kind: attribute
    of: escalation
    labels:
      en: Escalation from tier
    description: >-
      The support tier that hands the ticket on, when the escalation is from
      one tier to another.
    values-of: support-tier

  - id: escalation-to-tier
    kind: attribute
    of: escalation
    labels:
      en: Escalation to tier
    description: >-
      The support tier that receives the ticket. A notice to a manager may
      name a person instead of a tier; that person is not a support tier.
    values-of: support-tier

  - id: on-call-rotation
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: On-call rotation
    synonyms:
      en: [on-call schedule, pager rotation]
    description: >-
      The schedule of people responsible for answering alerts outside
      ordinary staffing. Any support tier can be on call, so the rotation is
      not a tier. It is not an alert and not an incident: it says who
      responds, not what failed.

  - id: on-call-rotation-team
    kind: attribute
    of: on-call-rotation
    labels:
      en: On-call rotation team
    description: The team whose members take turns on the rotation.
    values-of: team

  - id: on-call-rotation-participant
    kind: attribute
    of: on-call-rotation
    labels:
      en: On-call rotation participant
    description: >-
      A person who takes a turn on the rotation. Not every member of the
      team is on the rotation.
    values-of: person

  - id: alert
    kind: entity
    extends: observation
    labels:
      en: Alert
    synonyms:
      en: [monitoring alert]
    description: >-
      An observation that a stated condition was met and that a person may
      need to act. It does not by itself establish the cause, the severity,
      or that an incident has occurred: several alerts can be one incident,
      and an alert can be a false signal. An alert is not a log entry. A log
      entry is a line the software wrote; an alert may be raised because of
      log entries.

  - id: alert-affected-service
    kind: attribute
    of: alert
    labels:
      en: Alert affected service
    description: The service whose condition the alert is about.
    values-of: service

  - id: alert-acknowledged-at
    kind: attribute
    of: alert
    labels:
      en: Alert acknowledged at
    description: >-
      The instant a person accepts responsibility for the alert. Mean time
      to acknowledge ends at this instant and starts when the alert is
      raised. Acknowledgement is not restoration of the service.

  - id: alert-raised-at
    kind: attribute
    of: alert
    labels:
      en: Alert raised at
    description: >-
      The instant the alert was raised. Mean time to acknowledge runs from
      this instant until a person acknowledges the alert. Raising the alert
      is not restoration of the service.

  - id: runbook
    kind: entity
    extends: document
    labels:
      en: Runbook
    synonyms:
      en: [operations runbook]
    description: >-
      A document of steps for operating a service or for handling a known
      situation, written so a responder can follow it under pressure. It is
      not a postmortem, which records an incident after the fact, and not a
      known error, which records a cause and a workaround. The runbook may
      tell the reader to apply a workaround.

  - id: runbook-service
    kind: attribute
    of: runbook
    labels:
      en: Runbook service
    description: The service whose operation this runbook describes.
    values-of: service

  - id: postmortem
    kind: entity
    extends: document
    labels:
      en: Postmortem
    synonyms:
      en: [post-mortem, post-incident review, incident review]
    description: >-
      A document written after an incident, once the service is restored,
      recording what happened, how the service was restored, what
      contributed, and what will change. It is not the incident record, not
      the problem record, and not a runbook. A meeting about how a sprint
      went is a different practice and is not this document.

  - id: postmortem-incident
    kind: attribute
    of: postmortem
    labels:
      en: Postmortem incident
    description: >-
      The incident this document reviews. Writing the document does not
      reopen or replace the incident.
    values-of: incident

  - id: service-level-agreement
    kind: entity
    extends: contract
    labels:
      en: Service level agreement
    synonyms:
      en: [SLA, service-level agreement]
    description: >-
      A documented agreement between a service provider and a customer that
      identifies the services required and the expected level of service. It
      creates commitments between those parties, which is why it is a
      contract in this graph. It is not the target figure and not the
      measurement. Those are the service level objective and the service
      level indicator. The SRE book tells an agreement apart from an
      objective by the consequence of a miss, such as a service credit: a
      target with no consequence is an objective, even if someone calls it
      an agreement. An agreement between internal teams, with no customer,
      is not this concept. A target with no documented agreement is an
      objective.

  - id: service-level-agreement-service
    kind: attribute
    of: service-level-agreement
    labels:
      en: Service level agreement service
    description:       A service whose targets this agreement commits to.
    values-of: service

  - id: service-level-agreement-customer-organization
    kind: attribute
    of: service-level-agreement
    labels:
      en: Service level agreement customer organization
    description: >-
      An organization the agreement is with. This party defines the expected
      level with the provider. It need not be a purchasing customer.
    values-of: organization

  - id: service-level-agreement-customer-person
    kind: attribute
    of: service-level-agreement
    labels:
      en: Service level agreement customer person
    description: >-
      A person the agreement is with, when the customer is a person rather
      than an organization.
    values-of: person

  - id: service-level-objective
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Service level objective
    synonyms:
      en: [SLO, service-level objective]
    description: >-
      A target value, or a range of values, for a service level over a
      stated window, measured by a service level indicator. One shape is a
      lower bound on a success proportion, such as 99.9 percent of valid
      requests. Another is a bound on a duration. It is not the agreement
      that may promise the target, and not the measurement that shows
      whether the target was hit. An objective can exist with no service
      level agreement. If nothing has been agreed to happen when the target
      is missed, the target is an objective. ITIL's service level is a
      metric of expected or achieved quality and does not split the target
      from the measurement; this concept is only the target. The
      unreliability a success-proportion target still allows is the error
      budget.

  - id: service-level-objective-target
    kind: attribute
    of: service-level-objective
    labels:
      en: Service level objective target
    description: >-
      The target value over the objective's window, in the units of its
      indicator. A success proportion is a fraction of one: 99.9 percent is
      0.999, not 99.9. A duration target is a duration. It is not the
      measured value. The service level indicator defines that measurement.

  - id: service-level-objective-indicator
    kind: attribute
    of: service-level-objective
    labels:
      en: Service level objective indicator
    description: >-
      The service level indicator this objective sets a target against. The
      indicator is the measurement; the objective is the target.
    values-of: service-level-indicator

  - id: service-level-objective-agreement
    kind: attribute
    of: service-level-objective
    labels:
      en: Service level objective agreement
    description: >-
      A service level agreement that commits to this objective, when a
      customer is promised it. An internal objective can leave this empty.
    values-of: service-level-agreement

  - id: service-level-indicator
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Service level indicator
    synonyms:
      en: [SLI, service-level indicator]
    description: >-
      A quantitative measure of one aspect of the level of service provided,
      such as the proportion of well-formed requests that succeed, a latency,
      or the proportion of time the service is usable. Each indicator names
      its own good events and valid events. Because that definition changes
      from one indicator to another, the indicator is an entity here and not
      one measure with a single formula. An objective sets a target on an
      indicator. The indicator is not that target and not the agreement.
      Availability is one measure a team may adopt as an indicator. A key
      performance indicator judges success against a business objective and
      is a different concept. ITIL's service level names expected or achieved
      quality without this split; this concept is only the measurement.

  - id: service-level-indicator-service
    kind: attribute
    of: service-level-indicator
    labels:
      en: Service level indicator service
    description: The service whose behavior this indicator measures.
    values-of: service

  - id: availability
    kind: measure
    of: service
    labels:
      en: Availability
    synonyms:
      en: [service availability]
    description: >-
      The proportion of good events among valid events in a stated window,
      for the availability indicator the service uses. ITIL uses
      availability for the ability of a service to perform its agreed
      function when required. This concept is a measurement of that ability,
      not the ability itself and not a target. The SRE book defines it as
      the fraction of time a service is usable, and often implements that
      fraction as the yield of well-formed requests. Those two proportions
      answer different questions and must not be mixed in one figure. A
      duration of uptime is not this proportion. The proportion is not a
      service level objective and not a service level agreement.
    unit: proportion
    measure:
      formula: >-
        Good events divided by valid events in the window. The service level
        indicator states which events count. Counting time, good events are
        the time the service could perform its agreed function. Counting
        requests, good events are the successful well-formed requests and
        valid events are the well-formed requests. One figure uses one of
        those definitions. Recomputed for the window.
      aggregation: none

  - id: error-budget
    kind: measure
    of: service-level-objective
    labels:
      en: Error budget
    description: >-
      The unreliability a success-proportion service level objective allows
      in its window: one minus the target. A 99.9 percent objective allows
      0.1 percent. Applied to the valid events in the window, that
      proportion is a count of allowed bad events, which is the same
      allowance in the indicator's units. This measure is the allowance, not
      the amount still unused after failures have consumed some of it. It
      applies when the objective is a success proportion, the form the SRE
      workbook uses for an error budget. A target that is not a proportion,
      such as an average latency under a stated duration, is not turned into
      a budget by subtracting it from one. The budget is not a second
      objective and not availability.
    unit: proportion
    measure:
      formula: >-
        One minus the objective's success-proportion target, with that target
        as a fraction of one. The count of bad events allowed in the window
        is that proportion times the valid events in the window. Recomputed
        for the window.
      aggregation: none

  - id: sla-compliance-rate
    kind: measure
    of: service-level-agreement
    labels:
      en: SLA compliance rate
    description: >-
      The proportion of the targets in one service level agreement that were
      met in a stated window. This proportion is defined here so several
      targets can be counted together. It is not a term from ITIL or from
      the SRE book, not availability, and not an error budget. It does not
      say which missed target carries a consequence. Meeting each target is
      decided on that target's own indicator.
    unit: proportion
    measure:
      formula: >-
        The number of agreed service level targets met in the window,
        divided by the number of agreed targets that applied in the window.
        Recomputed for the window.
      aggregation: none

  - id: mean-time-to-restore
    kind: measure
    of: service
    labels:
      en: Mean time to restore
    synonyms:
      en: [MTRS, mean time to restore service]
    description: >-
      The arithmetic mean, over failures in a stated window, of the time
      from each failure until the service is restored. The failure instant
      is incident failure at when it is known, and restored is incident
      restored at: the service meets its success criterion again. That can
      differ from the time the incident record is marked resolved and from
      the time a person acknowledges an alert. ITIL calls this metric mean
      time to restore service (MTRS). The abbreviation MTTR is also used for
      mean time to repair, mean time to resolve, and mean time to recover,
      so it is not a synonym here. DORA's recovery metric is failed
      deployment recovery time. That metric covers only recovery from a
      failed deployment and is summarized as a median, not as this mean.
    measure:
      formula: >-
        The sum of the restore durations of the failures in the window,
        divided by the number of those failures. Each duration runs from the
        failure instant until the service is restored.
      aggregation: none

  - id: mean-time-to-acknowledge
    kind: measure
    labels:
      en: Mean time to acknowledge
    synonyms:
      en: [MTTA]
    description: >-
      The arithmetic mean, over alerts in a stated window, of the time from
      when an alert is raised until a person acknowledges it.
      Acknowledgement means someone accepts the alert. It is not restoration
      and not resolution of an incident. This mean is not mean time to
      restore, and it is not one of DORA's software delivery metrics.
    measure:
      formula: >-
        The sum of the times from each alert in the window, starting when it
        is raised, until a person acknowledges it, divided by the number of
        those alerts.
      aggregation: none

  - id: failed-deployment-recovery-time
    kind: measure
    labels:
      en: Failed deployment recovery time
    description: >-
      How long it takes to restore service after a deployment, or a release
      to users, degrades the service and needs intervention. This is DORA's
      recovery metric, alongside deployment frequency, lead time for
      changes, and change failure rate. Until 2023 DORA asked for time to
      restore service after any user-impacting incident, which mixed
      failures caused by a change with failures from elsewhere, and that
      older question is not a synonym of this metric. A measured window is
      summarized by the median of the recovery durations. DORA's survey asks
      for the typical time rather than publishing a mean. The arithmetic
      mean of restore times for failures in general is mean time to restore.
      The clock ends when the service is restored, not when an alert is
      acknowledged and not merely when the incident record is closed.
    measure:
      formula: >-
        The median, over failed deployments in the window, of the elapsed
        time from the deployment that caused the degradation until the
        service is restored. A failure that a deployment did not cause is
        excluded.
      aggregation: none
